+++ b/patches/README.md
@@ -16,6 +16,7 @@ Kernel changes are not here — they are commits in
 | `bluetooth_mtk_sniff_subrating.patch` | `packages/modules/Bluetooth` | The connsys controller advertises sniff-subrating support, then answers `HCI_Sniff_Subrating` (0x0811) with a Command Status event carrying `COMMAND_DISALLOWED` instead of the Command Complete the spec requires. AOSP asserts on the response *form* and aborts (`system/gd/hci/hci_layer.cc:278`), so `com.android.bluetooth` crash-loops and any audio link drops. Reproduced with AirPods Pro 3: crash within seconds of pressing play. Affects every sniff-subrating-capable headset, not one vendor. |
 | `fmradio_rds_lp64_struct.patch` | `packages/apps/FMRadio` | RDS never appears, on every station, because the FM JNI can never read it. `COM_read_rds_data()` accepts the read only if it returns exactly `sizeof(RDSData_Struct)`, but `struct rds_group_cnt` in `jni/fmr/fm.h` declares its 33 counters `unsigned long` where the kernel's `struct rds_group_cnt_t` uses `unsigned int`. On LP64 that makes the userspace struct 824 bytes against the kernel's 688, so the length check fails every time and `readRds()` reports no events forever. Only the arm64 JNI is installed, so the app always hits it; on a 32-bit build the sizes coincide, which is why it survived upstream. Also widens `rds_raw_data.data` to 148 so the declaration matches `struct rds_raw_t`. That one is cosmetic, not a fix: tail padding makes both structs 156 bytes either way, and `FM_IOCTL_RDS_GET_LOG` is declared but never called. |
 | `sensors_multihal_vendor_type.patch` | `hardware/interfaces` | Xiaomi's sensor stack emits vendor sensor type **75**, below `DEVICE_PRIVATE_BASE` (65536). Four places assert that anything unrecognised must be at or above that line, and `CHECK_GE` is `LOG(FATAL)`: `aidl/convert/convert.cpp:286` and `:493`, and `aidl/default/multihal/ConvertUtils.cpp:229` and `:411`. Each sits at the top of a `default:` arm whose body is a plain `memcpy` of the raw values, i.e. the code already handles an arbitrary type, so the assert guards nothing and only decides which process dies. `ConvertUtils.cpp` kills `android.hardware.sensors-service.xiaomi-multihal`, which init restarts into the same event, so every tombstone reads `Process uptime: 0s`. `convert.cpp:286` is worse: it runs inside **system_server**'s SensorService thread, so a single type-75 event aborts system_server and the whole UI restarts to the boot animation without the bootloader being involved. Observed 2026-08-21, with a `com.android.bluetooth` abort alongside it as collateral of the restart. Reported downstream as issue #1. |
+| `codec2_mtk_dequeue_fence_leak.patch` | `frameworks/av` | The MediaTek Codec2 HAL (`android.hardware.media.c2@1.2-mediatek`) leaks the release fence that `dequeueBuffer` hands it: one `sync_file` per displayed frame, never closed. At 30 fps it reaches the process's 32768-fd limit after about 18 minutes of video, then aborts (`HidlSupport: Failed to clone native_handle in hidl_handle: Too many open files`) and restarts, killing the app's decoder (`DEAD_OBJECT`). In YouTube Shorts this shows as the picture jumping to the next clip while the captions stay. The patch wraps the producer passed to the HAL in `client.cpp` so that `dequeueBuffer` waits on the fence on the framework side and gives the HAL no fence to leak. Verified 2026-11-22: HAL fd count flat at about 160 with no `sync_file` entries while Shorts play, and 20+ minutes of Shorts with no skip. |
 
 ## Upstreaming status
 
